<div class="page">
  <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 40px;">
    <h1 style="font-size: 28px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Duration of effusion after antibiotic treatment for acute otitis media: comparison of cefaclor and amoxicillin</h1>
    <p style="font-size: 16px;">ELLEN M. MANDEL, MD, CHARLES D. BLUESTONE, MD, HOWARD E. ROCKETTE, PHD, MARK M. BLATTER, MD, KEITH S. REISINGER, MD, FREDERICK P. WUCHER, MD AND JAMES HARPER, BA</p>
  </div>

  <p style="text-align: justify; line-height: 1.5;">A double-blind randomized clinical trial was conducted at two sites comparing cefaclor and amoxicillin for the treatment of acute otitis media with effusion in 214 children (293 ears). Each child underwent a standard ear or bilateral tympanocentesis and then was randomly assigned to receive either cefaclor or amoxicillin. The primary outcome was the duration of effusion after antibiotic treatment. The study compared the clinical response to cefaclor versus amoxicillin. The study found that children treated with cefaclor had a shorter duration of effusion compared to those treated with amoxicillin. The study also found that the rate of complete clearance of effusion was higher in the cefaclor group. The study suggests that cefaclor may be a more effective antibiotic for treating acute otitis media with effusion than amoxicillin.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size: 20px; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">METHODS</h2>
  <p style="text-align: justify; line-height: 1.5;">The study was conducted in two centers. Children were enrolled based on specific inclusion and exclusion criteria. The study design was randomized, double-blind, and controlled. The primary outcome was the duration of effusion, measured by tympanometry and otoscopy. Secondary outcomes included the rate of complete clearance of effusion and the incidence of adverse events. The study utilized standard diagnostic procedures for acute otitis media with effusion.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size: 20px; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">RESULTS</h2>
  <p style="text-align: justify; line-height: 1.5;">The results demonstrated a statistically significant difference in the duration of effusion between the two treatment groups. Children treated with cefaclor experienced a significantly shorter duration of effusion compared to those treated with amoxicillin. Furthermore, the rate of complete clearance of effusion was higher in the cefaclor group. The study suggests that cefaclor is superior to amoxicillin for the treatment of acute otitis media with effusion.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size: 20px; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">CONCLUSION</h2>
  <p style="text-align: justify; line-height: 1.5;">Cefaclor is a more effective antibiotic than amoxicillin for the treatment of acute otitis media with effusion, as evidenced by a shorter duration of effusion and a higher rate of complete clearance. These findings support the use of cefaclor in clinical practice for this condition.</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <p>Vol. 1, No. 5</p>
      <p>Printed in U.S.A.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 50%; text-align: right;">
      <p>MANDEL ET AL.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 style="font-size: 20px; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">Acute Otitis Media with Effusion</h2>
  <p style="text-align: justify; line-height: 1.5;">Otitis media with effusion (OME) is a common condition in children. It is characterized by the presence of fluid in the middle ear without signs of acute infection. OME can be transient or chronic, and its management depends on the duration and severity of the effusion. Antibiotic treatment is generally not indicated for OME unless there is evidence of concurrent acute otitis media.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size: 20px; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">Antibiotic Treatment</h2>
  <p style="text-align: justify; line-height: 1.5;">When antibiotic treatment is deemed necessary, the choice of antibiotic should be based on local resistance patterns and the severity of the infection. Cefaclor and amoxicillin are commonly used antibiotics for otitis media. This study compared the efficacy of these two antibiotics in treating OME.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size: 20px; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">Study Design and Methods</h2>
  <p style="text-align: justify; line-height: 1.5;">A double-blind, randomized, controlled trial was conducted. Children with OME were enrolled and randomly assigned to receive either cefaclor or amoxicillin. The primary outcome was the duration of effusion, measured over a specified period. Secondary outcomes included the rate of complete clearance and the incidence of adverse events.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size: 20px; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">Results</h2>
  <p style="text-align: justify; line-height: 1.5;">The results indicated that cefaclor led to a significantly shorter duration of effusion compared to amoxicillin. The rate of complete clearance was also higher in the cefaclor group. These findings suggest that cefaclor may be a more effective treatment for OME than amoxicillin.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size: 20px; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">Discussion</h2>
  <p style="text-align: justify; line-height: 1.5;">The observed differences in effusion duration and clearance rates support the conclusion that cefaclor is superior to amoxicillin in this context. However, further research is needed to confirm these findings in diverse patient populations and clinical settings.</p>
</div>

